WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:06.660
Imagine a world composed solely of a single,
endless desert.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.940
What extreme forms of life might we find here?

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:15.969
Would this be a world of microscopic organisms…
or of unfathomable giants?

00:00:15.969 --> 00:00:21.830
Arrakis, also called ‘Dune’ is the titular
planet at the center of the world-famous science

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:24.860
fiction book and recent film of the same name.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:30.930
And although the planet is a desert of intense
heat and extreme scarcity, it is also abundant

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.700
with unexpected life.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:37.980
So, join me in this tour of the ecosystems
of Arrakis, which will dive deep into the

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.360
extended material from the books and other
sources.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.579
We’ll uncover how such fantastical forms
of life might function, and find biological

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:50.579
analogues in the most unexpected places on
our own planet.

00:00:50.579 --> 00:00:54.640
So, let’s begin our expedition across Arrakis.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:58.120
Be sure to walk without rhythm…

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.769
Few places in the universe compare to Arrakis.

00:01:00.769 --> 00:01:07.170
In terms of precipitation, the planet is as
dry as the surface of Mars.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:12.329
Compared to such a world, Earth is a land
practically overflowing with water.

00:01:12.329 --> 00:01:17.630
Desert animals on Earth have evolved countless
strategies to survive the intense heat and

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:21.970
sand, but many of these adaptations will be
of little use on Arrakis.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:26.760
Yet according to the novel, virtually all
species on the planet of Arrakis were introduced

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:31.469
from elsewhere, so some familiar creatures
must be able to endure this climate.

00:01:31.469 --> 00:01:35.140
As we set off to find some… stay close.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:38.619
For the Desert takes the weak.

00:01:38.619 --> 00:01:44.060
Hopping across the sands, is a very special
form of hopping desert rodent.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:49.630
Known as Muad’Dib by the Freh-men populace,
the jumping rodent is an introduced species

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:51.530
from Earth called a Jerboa.

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:57.689
A creature that lives in the hottest regions
of North Africa and Asia, Jerboas are excellent

00:01:57.689 --> 00:02:04.800
fits for the brutal deserts of Arrakis — where
they have evolved an unexpected new behavior…

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:10.569
The Villeneuve film shows the Muad’Dib collecting
dew gathered on its ears for moisture.

00:02:10.569 --> 00:02:15.670
This behavior is highly convergent with the
survival strategy of one of the most extreme

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:20.450
desert lifeforms on Earth: the Namib Desert
Darkling Beetles.

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:25.700
On foggy desert days, these beetles collect
water droplets on their body’s surface thanks

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:29.459
to specialized hydrophilic bumps on their
forewings.

00:02:29.459 --> 00:02:35.190
This incredible behavior, known as ‘fog-basking,’
requires the beetles to stay motionless for

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:40.760
long periods in this posture — which allows
for the collected water to trickle down into

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:41.940
their mouths.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:47.250
Looking at these species’ hydrophilic zones
under magnification reveals just how advanced

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:49.409
their miniature moisture-farms have become.

00:02:49.409 --> 00:02:50.709
Truly, life can adapt to almost any environment…

00:02:50.709 --> 00:02:56.150
So, it is likely the ears of the Arrakeen
Muad’Dib rodent possess similar hydrophilic

00:02:56.150 --> 00:03:01.569
zones to facilitate the collection of water
from the atmosphere.

00:03:01.569 --> 00:03:06.989
In this extremely arid environment, very few
species of introduced plants have been able

00:03:06.989 --> 00:03:08.470
to take root.

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:15.000
Certain genuses of cacti are some of the only
florae that can endure the scorching Arakeen

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:16.000
sun.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.590
And aside from the Muad’Dib, only a small
number of Earth animals have managed to sustain

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:23.819
an existence in this land of scarcity.

00:03:23.819 --> 00:03:29.189
According to the book, a genus of desert hawks
has successfully survived as mid-sized predators,

00:03:29.189 --> 00:03:35.370
and an unspecified genus of desert foxes has
also made their home here, filling a similar

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:36.599
niche.

00:03:36.599 --> 00:03:41.849
But these predators are comically low on the
food-chain, compared to the largest and most

00:03:41.849 --> 00:03:44.040
famous lifeform on Arrakis.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:50.990
No species holds more power or commands more
awe than the mighty Sandworm — a creature

00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:54.070
of endless biological mysteries.

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:59.040
The common name of these colossi calls back
to the tiny worms of earth, although the two

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.390
share no genetic relation.

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:06.890
While Sandworms and earthworms are both lifeforms
with tube-shaped body-plans that burrow underground

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:11.970
in search of organic matter — the main difference
comes, of course, in size.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:19.250
Earthworms average a few inches or centimeters
long, whereas sandworms can reach 1,400 feet,

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:21.979
or 450 meters in length…

00:04:21.979 --> 00:04:27.220
In sheer volume, the closest thing we have
on earth to a sandworm are the largest species

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:28.240
of whales.

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:33.750
And in the Villeneuve film especially, sandworms
seem to have a lot in common with whales,

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:36.910
with their teeth almost looking like baleen.

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:42.220
This makes a lot of sense, as in whales baleen
bristles are highly effective at filtering

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:47.600
the vast schools of tiny organisms that whales
feed on to reach their ridiculous sizes.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:52.910
And we know from the Dune books that Sandworms
have a very similar food source, which helps

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:56.100
make their absurd sizes a bit more realistic…

00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:00.500
We’ll discuss the surprising food source
of the Sandworms soon.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:05.180
For now, there’s another great mystery of
the Sandworms we need to solve.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:08.270
How could a creature ‘swim’ through the
sand?

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:13.729
Sand as we know it is made up of granular
material, not exactly good for swimming through.

00:05:13.729 --> 00:05:19.350
Well, it’s possible the sandworms are able
to make the sand of Arrakis behave more like

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:22.360
water through a process called fluidization.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:28.190
The concept of fluidizing a granular material
like sand might sound like science fiction,

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:33.580
but scientists have long been able to achieve
this by introducing gas, which surprisingly

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:35.660
makes sand behave like a liquid.

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:39.110
And it’s not just scientists who have figured
this out.

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:44.030
When earthly sand-swimming animals like the
wedge-snouted skink burrow, their movements

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:47.960
create more fluid-like sand that is easier
to move through.

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:53.210
Director Villeneuve implies that sandworms
swim through the dunes of Arrakis via a similar

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:58.660
process by having the sand subtly liquify
before they surface.

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:04.410
Notably, Sandworms can also sense rhythmic
vibrations and irregular shifts on the surface

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:10.699
of the sand, and aggressively pursue the origin
of such vibrations to defend their territory.

00:06:10.699 --> 00:06:16.319
Once again, there are numerous surprising
examples in nature of animals that can sense

00:06:16.319 --> 00:06:21.880
vibration to track other lifeforms, including
snakes — and, perhaps more surprisingly,

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:22.880
frogs.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:28.569
But the most intriguing example for our purposes
are spiders, which can detect tremors caused

00:06:28.569 --> 00:06:31.300
by panicking prey caught in their web.

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:36.310
Spiders can detect these subtle vibrations
thanks to mechanoreceptory organs on their

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:39.060
exoskeletons called Slit Sensilla.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:44.230
It’s possible that Sandworms possess their
own mechanoreceptory organs that allow them

00:06:44.230 --> 00:06:46.300
to ‘hear’ vibrations on the surface.

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:50.669
And when a Sandworm surfaces, it’s quite
the spectacle.

00:06:50.669 --> 00:06:56.069
As a result of static electricity being released
into positively charged air, dry lightning

00:06:56.069 --> 00:06:58.740
illuminates the Arrakeen sky.

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:04.060
Seeing a sandworm breaching would likely be
like witnessing the approach of a deity.

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:07.139
And some Sandworms might be even more impressive.

00:07:07.139 --> 00:07:14.530
There are rumors in the novel of worms that
grow up to 2,000 to 3,000 feet, or 700 to

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:17.460
1000 meters, in the southern polar regions,
although this could not be confirmed.

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:18.460
If such creatures are hiding on Arrakis, they’d
be the largest creatures I’ve ever covered

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:20.449
on this channel… bigger than two Empire
State Buildings—put together.

00:07:20.449 --> 00:07:23.080
So what are these creatures’ food source?

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:30.650
To find the abundant life that feeds the great
Sandworm, one must look under a microscope.

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:35.860
The lower sands of Arrakis are filled with
planktonic life, which the Sandworm feeds

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:36.860
on.

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:41.520
But what’s most bizarre is that these tiny
organisms — called Sandplankton — are

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:44.660
actually the larval stage of the Sandworms!

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:50.580
That’s right, the sandworm feeds on members
of their own species, and are both the foundation

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:54.389
and top predators of the Arakeen ecosystem.

00:07:54.389 --> 00:07:58.300
But is such a bizarre, cyclical food-chain
even possible?

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:01.729
Once again, a possible answer lies in nature…

00:08:01.729 --> 00:08:06.990
Studies of North American Tiger Salamanders
have uncovered a highly bizarre and somewhat

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:08.509
similar life-cycle.

00:08:08.509 --> 00:08:14.949
Most Tiger Salamander Larva are small creatures
with round, almost nonexistent teeth which

00:08:14.949 --> 00:08:17.400
proliferate in large numbers.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:23.570
On rare occasions, however, some larva morph
into larger organisms, with wider mouths and

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:28.710
enlarged teeth — and honestly, they look
a bit like Sandworms from certain angles.

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:35.050
And like the Sandworms, these predators feed
primarily on the smaller larvae of their own

00:08:35.050 --> 00:08:36.050
kind.

00:08:36.050 --> 00:08:42.350
And there is one other stage of the Sandworms
lifecycle: the blob-like, intermediary form

00:08:42.350 --> 00:08:45.580
of the Sandtrout — or the ‘little makers.’

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:51.340
Although these slow, amoebic forms might not
seem significant, their biological processes

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:57.180
naturally create one of the most important
substances in the Dune universe: the Spice

00:08:57.180 --> 00:08:58.530
Melange.

00:08:58.530 --> 00:09:02.900
In the Dune universe, the spice is one of
the most valuable substances due to its effect

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:08.310
on the human mind — unlocking the key to
interstellar navigation and having so much

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:10.440
sway it can topple empires.

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:16.920
So, it might sound unusual that it comes from
a routine process of an organism like a Sandtrout.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:22.670
Yet numerous earth animals — from frogs
to sea slugs — naturally produce concentrations

00:09:22.670 --> 00:09:27.260
of unique chemicals with valuable medicinal
and scientific applications.

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:32.150
Granted, the sci-fi powers of the Spice are
far more extreme…

00:09:32.150 --> 00:09:35.240
But Arrakis is a planet of extremes.

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:40.500
It is a world where the very smallest and
very largest lifeforms play a role in the

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:43.420
destiny of all who venture into the desert.

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:49.390
And although we’re at the end of our investigation
of this perilous and mysterious world, remember

00:09:49.390 --> 00:09:53.430
the great desert can never be understood in
its entirety...

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:56.450
As always, thanks for watching.

00:09:56.450 --> 00:10:00.990
If you enjoyed this entry, please help make
this work possible by liking, subscribing,

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:05.990
and hitting the notification icon to stay
up to date on all things Curious.

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:07.140
See you in the next video.

